;;
;; REPL/RedTeam/[RT-H]_InputDomain.repl — canonical integration layout (OuronetInformational/ARCHITECTURE/REPL_AND_TESTS.md)
;;   Inter-tx:   ;;|| NEXT >  (same three lines as REPL/Stage_01/[2.2]_Core.repl)
;;   Intra-tx:   ;;==== <TX label> · mm · <slug> ====  + next-line (print "--- [<label> · mm · …] ---"); mm = 01.. within each (begin-tx …) (see OuronetInformational/ARCHITECTURE/REPL_AND_TESTS.md).
;;   format "{}": Talos / module echoes and (env-gas) — document additional prefixes in this file's (print …) lines as needed.
;;
(print "")
(print "================================================================")
(print "FILE  REPL/RedTeam/[RT-H]_InputDomain.repl")
(print "================================================================")
(print "Legend (terminal observability): see ;; preface; echoes use <(…)> where helpful.")
(print "Source: ;;|| NEXT > between txs; ;;==== … · mm · … ==== + banner before each begin-tx.")
(print "")
;;
;; REPL/RedTeam/[RT-H]_InputDomain.repl — FAMILY H: input domain.
;;
;; "What happens at the edges of what an argument is allowed to be?"
;;
;; Deliberately NOT a fuzzing campaign. A full gate run is ~7 minutes, so random search is an
;; expensive way to find what reading the guards tells you for free. These attacks are targeted at
;; specific gaps identified by reading `SWPU|X>SWAP`, which validates its inputs one at a time and
;; therefore cannot see any property OF THE SET.
;;
;; Run: cd REPL && pact "RedTeam/[RT-H]_InputDomain.repl"
;;
(load "../deploy-stage00.repl")
(load "../deploy-stage01.repl")
(load "../Stage_01/[6.2]_DPTF.repl")
(load "../Stage_01/[6.3]_SWP.repl")

;;|| NEXT >
;;
(begin-tx "RT-H-001 - swap a token for itself, and swap a token twice")
(env-gasmodel "table")(env-gaslimit 100000000)(env-gas 0)
(namespace "ouronet-ns")
;;==== RT-H-001 · 01 · set properties the per-item validator cannot see ====
(print "--- [RT-H-001 · 01 · set properties the per-item validator cannot see] ---")
;;<<RT-H-001>> FAMILY: H | STATUS: FIXED
;;HYPOTHESIS: `SWPU|X>SWAP` -- the capability guarding the NO-SLIPPAGE swap path, which is the one a
;;            caller reaches by passing the `-1.0` sentinel -- validates the output is on the pool,
;;            that swapping is enabled, that input-ids and input-amounts are the same LENGTH, and
;;            then loops over the inputs checking each is on the pool with a valid amount. Every one
;;            of those is a per-ITEM property. Nothing checked a property of the SET. So two things
;;            should be constructible: `output-id` also appearing in `input-ids` (swap a token for
;;            itself), and the same id appearing twice (pay one token, be credited for two).
;;METHOD:     drive both through the live Talos client on a real pool, and record WHICH guard
;;            refuses. The identity of the refusing guard is the entire result here.
;;RESULT:     both were constructible, and both were refused -- but by guards that were NOT about
;;            the set. Fixed 2026-09-14 on the owner's ruling; this block now pins the real rules.
;;
;;WHAT USED TO REFUSE THE SELF-SWAP, and it was not a validator. `output-id` in `input-ids` passed
;;every check in `SWPU|X>SWAP`: it is on the pool, its amount is positive, the lengths match. The
;;swap proceeded INTO the curve math, which computed an output of exactly ZERO -- adding and
;;removing the same token on a constant-function curve nets to nothing -- and the transfer of that
;;zero was then refused by `DPTF::UEV_Amount`'s `(> amount 0.0)`. The transaction was rejected by a
;;ZERO-AMOUNT TRANSFER RULE, three modules away from the mistake, and only because the arithmetic
;;happened to land on exactly 0.0.
;;
;;    That was the finding. The system was not protected from self-swaps by a rule forbidding them;
;;    it was protected by the curve returning zero. Any future change that made a self-swap return
;;    a non-zero positive amount -- a different curve, an amplifier, a fee rebate, a rounding
;;    direction -- would have turned this into value creation with no guard in the way.
;;
;;WHAT USED TO REFUSE THE DUPLICATE was also not a uniqueness rule: "Only a single Input can be used
;;in Stable Swap" is a POOL-TYPE rule. It happened to make duplicates unconstructible on a STABLE
;;pool because stable pools take one input at all. It said nothing about weighted pools, which
;;accept several inputs by design -- so the duplicate case was pinned only for the stable shape and
;;the weighted shape was an open follow-up.
;;
;;THE FIX. `SWPU|X>SWAP` now states both rules directly, as properties of the SET:
;;
;;    (enforce (not (contains output-id input-ids)) "Output Token ... cannot also be an Input ...")
;;    (ref-U|LST::UEV_IzUnique input-ids)
;;
;;Both now refuse IN THE CAPABILITY, before any debit and before the curve is evaluated. That is
;;the difference this block measures: not merely that the attacks fail, but that they fail EARLY
;;and for a reason that names them. The old defences remain in place behind these; they are simply
;;no longer the thing standing between a malformed set and the pool.
;;==== RT-H-001 - swap a token for itself, and swap a token twice · 01 · env-sigs (caps) ==== =====
(print "--- [RT-H-001 - swap a token for itself, and swap a token twice · 01 · env-sigs (caps)] ---")
(env-sigs [{ "key": "PK_AncientHodler", "caps": [] }])
;;==== RT-H-001 - swap a token for itself, and swap a token twice · 02 · let / invocation ==== ====
(print "--- [RT-H-001 - swap a token for itself, and swap a token twice · 02 · let / invocation] ---")
(let
    (
        (ref-TS01-C3:module{TalosStageOne_ClientThreeV4} TS01-C3)
        (ref-DPTF:module{DemiourgosPactTrueFungibleV2} DPTF)
        (ref-SWP:module{SwapperV4} SWP)
        (ref-SWPU:module{SwapperUsageV3} SWPU)
        (pool:string "S|DLK-98c486052a51|OURO-98c486052a51|DWK-98c486052a51")
        ;;the WEIGHTED twin of the same three tokens, issued by [6.3] at SWP|TX 04. Same tokens,
        ;;different pool type -- so the only thing that differs between attack 2 and attack 3 is
        ;;the rule that gets to answer.
        (wpool:string "W|DLK-98c486052a51|OURO-98c486052a51|DWK-98c486052a51")
        (tok:string "OURO-98c486052a51")
        (other:string "DLK-98c486052a51")
        (me:string KST.ANHD)
    )
    (let
        (
            (mine-before:decimal (ref-DPTF::UR_AccountSupply tok me))
            (pool-before:[decimal] (ref-SWP::UR_PoolTokenSupplies pool))
        )
        (map print
            [
                (format "<<RT-H-001>> holding {} {} against pool {}" [mine-before tok pool-before])
                ;;ATTACK 1 -- swap a token for ITSELF. Now refused by the SET rule that names it,
                ;;in SWPU|X>SWAP, rather than by a zero-amount transfer rule three modules away.
                (expect-failure "<<RT-H-001>> a self-swap is refused by the rule that forbids it"
                    "cannot also be an Input Token"
                    (ref-TS01-C3::SWP|C_MultiSwapNoSlippage me me pool [tok] [100.0] tok))
                ;;ATTACK 2 -- the same input id twice, on the STABLE pool. Still refused by the
                ;;POOL-TYPE rule, and the reason is worth more than the fix was: the new uniqueness
                ;;check never gets to speak here, because the pool-type rule fires UPSTREAM OF THE
                ;;CAPABILITY ENTIRELY. TS01-C3::SWP|C_MultiSwapNoSlippage binds
                ;;
                ;;    (slippage-bounds ... (ref-SWPU::UDC_SpawnSlippageBounds swpair ...))
                ;;
                ;;in a `let` BEFORE it calls SWPU::C_Swap, and a Pact `let` is EAGER -- so the
                ;;curve runs, and the curve's own (= l1 1) stable-pool enforce aborts, before
                ;;SWPU|X>SWAP is ever composed. The validator is downstream of the math it guards.
                ;;The next assertion proves that claim rather than asserting it.
                (expect-failure "<<RT-H-001>> on a STABLE pool the pool-type rule still answers first"
                    "Only a single Input can be used in Stable Swap"
                    (ref-TS01-C3::SWP|C_MultiSwapNoSlippage me me pool [other other] [50.0 50.0] tok))
                ;;WHERE that refusal comes from, demonstrated: the same message falls out of the
                ;;slippage-bounds constructor on its own, with no client call and no capability in
                ;;scope. That is the eager `let` in Talos, not a guard in SWPU.
                (expect-failure "<<RT-H-001>> ...and it comes from Talos's EAGER let, not from the swap capability"
                    "Only a single Input can be used in Stable Swap"
                    (ref-SWPU::UDC_SpawnSlippageBounds pool [other other] [50.0 50.0] tok -1.0))
                ;;ATTACK 3 -- the same input twice on a WEIGHTED pool, which the original pass
                ;;recorded as an OPEN FOLLOW-UP because this block could not reach one. A weighted
                ;;pool accepts several inputs by design, so the pool-type rule has nothing to say
                ;;and the curve does not object. This is the case that had NO guard at all before
                ;;2026-09-14, and it is now refused by uniqueness -- by name.
                (expect-failure "<<RT-H-001>> on a WEIGHTED pool, where nothing objected before, uniqueness answers"
                    "Unique Items Required"
                    (ref-TS01-C3::SWP|C_MultiSwapNoSlippage me me wpool [other other] [50.0 50.0] tok))
                ;;AND NOW "NOTHING MOVED" CAN BE ASSERTED, which it could not be before, and the
                ;;change is the point of the repair rather than a bonus.
                ;;
                ;;RULE 9 says `expect-failure` catches the abort MID-TRANSACTION and does NOT roll
                ;;back writes that already happened. When this block pinned the OLD behaviour, two
                ;;such assertions were written and both FAILED, showing the caller debited by
                ;;exactly the 100.0 self-swap input and the pool's OURO reserve up 0.53:
                ;;
                ;;    caller  389170.206086... -> 389070.206086...
                ;;    pool    [.., 901.591515.., ..] -> [.., 902.121515.., ..]
                ;;
                ;;They were removed as unassertable. They are assertable NOW, and they pass, for a
                ;;reason that is itself the result: the new guards live in the CAPABILITY, so the
                ;;refusal happens before the first write instead of after the debit and the curve.
                ;;RULE 9 has not changed -- there is simply nothing left for it to expose.
                (expect "<<RT-H-001>> the refusal is now EARLY: the caller is not debited at all"
                    mine-before (ref-DPTF::UR_AccountSupply tok me))
                (expect "<<RT-H-001>> ...and the pool reserves are untouched by both attempts"
                    pool-before (ref-SWP::UR_PoolTokenSupplies pool))
                ;;NON-VACUITY: a well-formed swap on this same pool DOES work, so the refusals above
                ;;are about the malformed input sets and not about the pool being unusable.
                (expect "<<RT-H-001>> ...while a well-formed swap on the same pool succeeds"
                    true (> (length (ref-TS01-C3::SWP|C_SingleSwapNoSlippage me me pool other 10.0 tok)) 0))
            ]
        )
    )
    ""
)
(env-sigs [])
(rollback-tx)
;;||>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>
;;|| NEXT                   >
;;||>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>
(begin-tx "RT-H-002 - hand-build the Slippage object the constructor would refuse")
(env-gasmodel "table")(env-gaslimit 100000000)(env-gas 0)
(namespace "ouronet-ns")
;;==== RT-H-002 · 01 · a bound that lives in the constructor, not the consumer ====
(print "--- [RT-H-002 · 01 · a bound that lives in the constructor, not the consumer] ---")
;;<<RT-H-002>> FAMILY: H | STATUS: FIXED
;;HYPOTHESIS: this is Stage 0's V-01, the last of its three first-rank targets -- "slippage
;;            unbounded in BOTH directions; the @doc's '<= 50' is UI policy the chain never runs".
;;            `UDC_SpawnSmartSwapSlippageBounds` enforces
;;            `(or (= slippage -1.0) (and (> slippage 0.0) (<= slippage 50.0)))`, and its own @doc
;;            says it is "Called by the UI to generate the slippage-bounds object before submitting
;;            the Smart Swap transaction". But the CLIENT SURFACE takes the built OBJECT:
;;            `SWP|CC_SmartSwapWithSlippage (patron account input-id input-amount output-id
;;            slippage-bounds)`. Talos then does
;;            `(slippage:decimal (at "slippage-percent" slippage-bounds))` -- it reads the number
;;            back OUT of the caller's object. So if nothing downstream re-checks it, the ceiling is
;;            advisory: an integrator who hand-builds the object is not obliged to call the
;;            constructor at all.
;;METHOD:     assert the constructor DOES refuse an out-of-range value, then feed a hand-built
;;            object carrying that same value to the consumer that actually derives the bounds,
;;            `UC_SlippageMinMax`, and see what it computes. Pure compute on both sides, so the
;;            result is arithmetic rather than circumstance.
;;RESULT:     the ceiling is not enforced anywhere the value is USED. `UEV_SwapData` validates token
;;            sets, lengths and output-not-in-inputs and says nothing about slippage; a grep for
;;            `enforce` beside `slippage` in 19_SWPU.pact returns ONE hit and it is a doc comment.
;;            Above 100% the derived `min` goes NEGATIVE, which makes the bound a no-op -- every
;;            possible output passes. That is what the <= 50 ceiling exists to prevent.
(let
    (
        (ref-SWPU:module{SwapperUsageV3} SWPU)
        ;;hand-built, never passed through the constructor. This is the whole attack: the schema is
        ;;public and the client surface accepts the object, so nothing obliges a caller to use
        ;;UDC_SpawnSmartSwapSlippageBounds at all.
        (forged:object{SwapperUsageV3.Slippage}
            { "expected-output-amount" : 1000.0
            , "output-precision"       : 12
            , "slippage-percent"       : 9999.0 })
        (conforming:object{SwapperUsageV3.Slippage}
            { "expected-output-amount" : 1000.0
            , "output-precision"       : 12
            , "slippage-percent"       : 10.0 })
    )
    (let
        (
            (forged-mm:[decimal]     (ref-SWPU::UC_SlippageMinMax forged))
            (conforming-mm:[decimal] (ref-SWPU::UC_SlippageMinMax conforming))
        )
        (map print
            [
                (format "<<RT-H-002>> forged 9999%% -> min={} max={} | conforming 10%% -> min={} max={}"
                    [(at 0 forged-mm) (at 1 forged-mm) (at 0 conforming-mm) (at 1 conforming-mm)])
                ;;1] THE CONSTRUCTOR DOES ITS JOB. Without this the rest proves nothing -- a ceiling
                ;;   that is enforced nowhere at all is a different (smaller) finding than one that
                ;;   is enforced in the wrong place.
                (expect-failure "<<RT-H-002>> the constructor REFUSES 9999% -- the rule exists"
                    "Slippage must be greater than 0.0 and maximum 50.0, or -1.0 for no slippage"
                    (ref-SWPU::UDC_SpawnSmartSwapSlippageBounds
                        "AKOSON-98c486052a51" 5.0 "TUSD-98c486052a51" 9999.0))
                ;;2] ...AND THE CONSUMER DOES NOT. The same value, in a hand-built object, is
                ;;   accepted and turned into bounds without complaint.
                (expect "<<RT-H-002>> ...but the CONSUMER accepts the same value from a hand-built object"
                    2 (length forged-mm))
                ;;3] THE CONSEQUENCE, and it is arithmetic: above 100% the floor goes NEGATIVE, so
                ;;   no output can ever breach it. The bound is not merely loose, it is inoperative.
                (expect "<<RT-H-002>> a forged 9999% drives the slippage FLOOR below zero"
                    true (< (at 0 forged-mm) 0.0))
                ;;4] NON-VACUITY. A conforming 10% keeps the floor positive and tight, so assertion
                ;;   3 is about the forged value and not about UC_SlippageMinMax always going
                ;;   negative.
                (expect "<<RT-H-002>> ...while a conforming 10% keeps the floor positive"
                    true (> (at 0 conforming-mm) 0.0))
                ;;5] and the floor is exactly expected*(1 - sp/100), so the mechanism is pinned
                ;;   rather than inferred from the sign alone.
                (expect "<<RT-H-002>> the floor is expected*(1 - sp/100), pinned exactly"
                    900.0 (at 0 conforming-mm))
                ;;6] THE FIX (2026-09-15). `UEV_Slippage` now carries the constructor's rule and is
                ;;   called by all four `*-WITH-SLIPPAGE` defcaps, which previously took `slippage`
                ;;   as a parameter and validated nothing about it. Module-only, so no interface
                ;;   bump and no cascade. Pinned BY MESSAGE, so the refusal is attributable.
                (expect-failure "<<RT-H-002>> FIXED: the consumer-side validator refuses 9999%"
                    "Slippage must be greater than 0.0 and maximum 50.0, or -1.0 for no slippage"
                    (SWPU.UEV_Slippage 9999.0))
                ;;7] ...and it refuses the OTHER direction too -- V-01 said "unbounded in BOTH
                ;;   directions", and a zero or negative percent is just as inoperative.
                (expect-failure "<<RT-H-002>> ...and 0.0, which would make min=max=expected"
                    "Slippage must be greater than 0.0 and maximum 50.0, or -1.0 for no slippage"
                    (SWPU.UEV_Slippage 0.0))
                ;;8] NON-VACUITY, both arms. A validator that refuses everything is not a bound, it
                ;;   is a broken function -- so the conforming value and the documented -1.0
                ;;   sentinel must both still pass.
                (expect "<<RT-H-002>> ...while a conforming 10% passes"
                    true (!= "" (format "{}" [(SWPU.UEV_Slippage 10.0)])))
                (expect "<<RT-H-002>> ...and the -1.0 no-slippage sentinel still passes"
                    true (!= "" (format "{}" [(SWPU.UEV_Slippage -1.0)])))
            ]
        )
    )
)
(print "===^^^===")
(commit-tx)

;;|| NEXT >
;;
(begin-tx "RT-H-003 - the BAR sentinel as a table key, again")
(env-gasmodel "table")(env-gaslimit 100000000)(env-gas 0)
(namespace "ouronet-ns")
;;==== RT-H-003 · 01 · a sentinel that is not a key ====
(print "--- [RT-H-003 · 01 · a sentinel that is not a key] ---")
;;<<RT-H-003>> FAMILY: H | STATUS: FIXED
;;HYPOTHESIS: `UR_HotRewardBearingToken` returns the BAR sentinel ("|") when an ATS pair has no
;;            Hot-RBT -- nine of the fifteen live pairs are in that state. `C_HotRecovery` binds
;;            `h-rbt` from it and, in the SAME eager `let` group, does
;;            `(UR_NoncesUsed h-rbt)` -- a DPOF properties read keyed by "|". The bindings are
;;            evaluated BEFORE `with-capability`, so no guard in `ATS|C>HOT_RECOVERY` can run first.
;;METHOD:     drive the real client entrypoint at a pair with no Hot-RBT and pin the refusal by
;;            message. Then CONSTRUCT the harder state -- the pool owner switches hot recovery ON
;;            while the pair still has no Hot-RBT, which `ATS|S>SWITCH-HOT-RECOVERY` permits because
;;            it checks only `CAP_Owner` and the previous toggle value -- and drive it again.
;;RESULT:     CONFIRMED, then FIXED. Both calls died with
;;            `No value found in table ouronet-ns.DPOF_DPOF|T|Properties for key: |` -- through the
;;            real Talos client, on a pair that exists at deploy, and in the owner-constructed state
;;            too. Repaired in 10_ATSU.pact by hoisting `with-capability` above the binding group
;;            (the C_Recover repair, re-applied) and adding a Hot-RBT presence check to
;;            `ATS|C>HOT_RECOVERY`, because the toggle and the Hot-RBT are independent.
;;            The hoist is visible in the test itself: block 01 now needs a signature, because once
;;            the capability runs first `CAP_EnforceAccountOwnership` answers before any business
;;            rule -- authorisation before validation, as ruled.
;;
;;THIS EXACT BUG WAS FIXED TWENTY LINES BELOW, THREE DAYS EARLIER. `C_Recover` carries a comment
;;block explaining that its capability was moved ABOVE its `let` on 2026-09-12 because
;;
;;    "for a token that is not reward-bearing `UR_RewardBearingToken` returns the BAR sentinel, so
;;     the next binding looked up ATS pair `|` and died with
;;     `No value found in table ouronet-ns.ATS_ATS|Pairs for key: |`."
;;
;;Same sentinel, same eager-`let` ordering, same error shape, different table -- and the sibling
;;immediately above it was left alone. The repair here is the one that was already written once.
;;
;;==== RT-H-003 - the BAR sentinel as a table key, again · 01 · env-sigs (caps) ==== ==============
(print "--- [RT-H-003 - the BAR sentinel as a table key, again · 01 · env-sigs (caps)] ---")
(env-sigs [ { "key": "PK_AncientHodler", "caps": [] } ])
;;==== RT-H-003 - the BAR sentinel as a table key, again · 02 · let / invocation ==== =============
(print "--- [RT-H-003 - the BAR sentinel as a table key, again · 02 · let / invocation] ---")
(let
    (
        (pool:string "Bisthanium-98c486052a51")
        (ok-pool:string "PlebeicStrength-98c486052a51")
    )
    (map print
        [
            ;;Precondition, measured: the getter really does hand back the separator.
            (expect
                (format "<<RT-H-003a>> {} has NO Hot-RBT -- the getter returns the BAR sentinel" [pool])
                [false "|"]
                [ (ouronet-ns.ATS.URC_IzPresentHotRBT pool)
                  (ouronet-ns.ATS.UR_HotRewardBearingToken pool) ])
            ;;The control: a pool that HAS one. Without this the guards below could be refusing
            ;;every pool for some unrelated reason.
            (expect
                (format "<<RT-H-003b>> {} HAS one -- so the predicate discriminates" [ok-pool])
                true (ouronet-ns.ATS.URC_IzPresentHotRBT ok-pool))
            ;;With the toggle OFF the capability's own state check must answer -- which it can only
            ;;do if the capability is acquired BEFORE the binding group. The signature above is
            ;;required for the same reason and is itself evidence the hoist landed: once the cap
            ;;runs first, `CAP_EnforceAccountOwnership` answers before any business rule, per the
            ;;owner ruling that authorisation precedes validation. Unsigned, this now returns a
            ;;keyset failure rather than a table error -- which is the whole point.
            (expect-failure
                "<<RT-H-003c>> hot recovery on a pair with none is refused by a GUARD, not by a table read"
                "must be set to true for exec"
                (let ((r:module{TalosStageOne_ClientTwoV2} TS01-C2))
                    (r::ATS|C_HotRecovery KST.ANHD KST.ANHD pool 1.0)))
            ;;THE PREVIEW IS A SEPARATE COPY OF THE BUG. Fixing the exec path did NOT fix this:
            ;;`URCi_HotRecovery` has its own eager `let` with the same two lines, so a QUOTE still
            ;;died with the raw table error after `C_HotRecovery` was clean. A preview is what a UI
            ;;calls BEFORE it submits anything -- it must refuse in the same words as the op.
            ;;
            ;;THE EXPECTED MESSAGE HERE CHANGED ONCE, AND THE GATE IS WHY. It first read
            ;;"has no Hot-RBT", because that was the only guard the preview had. Establishing
            ;;refusal parity (family K) then put the TOGGLE check first in the preview, to match the
            ;;order ATS|C>HOT_RECOVERY uses -- and this line went red, catching a contradiction
            ;;between two of this suite's own assertions. Parity is the stronger requirement: with
            ;;the toggle off, the op says the toggle, so the preview must say the toggle. The
            ;;Hot-RBT wording is now pinned in block 02, where the toggle is ON and the op says it.
            (expect-failure
                "<<RT-H-003f>> the INFO cost PREVIEW refuses in the EXEC's words, not with a table error"
                "must be set to true for exec"
                (ouronet-ns.INFO-ONE.INFO_ATS|HotRecovery KST.ANHD KST.ANHD pool 1.0))
        ])
    ""
)
(env-sigs [ { "key": "PK_AncientHodler", "caps": [] } ])
;;==== RT-H-003 · 02 · the state the pool OWNER can construct ====
(print "--- [RT-H-003 · 02 · the state the pool OWNER can construct] ---")
;;ANHD owns this pair, and ATS|S>SWITCH-HOT-RECOVERY checks only CAP_Owner plus the previous toggle
;;value -- it does NOT require a Hot-RBT to exist. So the owner can switch recovery ON for a pair
;;that has none, and every caller then meets the raw DPOF read.
(let
    (
        (pool:string "Bisthanium-98c486052a51")
        (r:module{TalosStageOne_ClientTwoV2} TS01-C2)
    )
    (r::ATS|C_SwitchHotRecovery KST.ANHD (ATS.UR_OwnerKonto pool) pool true)
    (map print
        [
            (expect
                "<<RT-H-003d>> the owner CAN switch hot recovery on for a pair with no Hot-RBT"
                true (ouronet-ns.ATS.UR_ToggleHotRecovery pool))
            (expect-failure
                "<<RT-H-003e>> ...and hot recovery must STILL refuse by name, not by table key"
                "has no Hot-RBT"
                (r::ATS|C_HotRecovery KST.ANHD KST.ANHD pool 1.0))
            ;;Parity in the state that actually reaches the Hot-RBT guard: with the toggle ON the
            ;;op names the missing Hot-RBT, so the preview must name it too.
            (expect-failure
                "<<RT-H-003g>> ...and the PREVIEW names it too, in the same words as the op"
                "has no Hot-RBT"
                (ouronet-ns.INFO-ONE.INFO_ATS|HotRecovery KST.ANHD KST.ANHD pool 1.0))
        ])
    ;;Leave the pair as it was found.
    (r::ATS|C_SwitchHotRecovery KST.ANHD (ATS.UR_OwnerKonto pool) pool false)
    ""
)
(env-sigs [])
(print "")
(print "===^^^===")
(commit-tx)
